Let the roots of the polynomial $x^3+ax^2+bx+6$ be $r_1, r_2, r_3$. By Vieta's formulas, we have:
\begin{align*} \label{eq:1} r_1+r_2+r_3 &= -a \\ r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3 &= b \\ r_1r_2r_3 &= -6\end{aligned}
Since $r_1, r_2, r_3$ are distinct integers, we need to find all possible sets of distinct integer roots whose product is $-6$. The possible sets are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\{1, 2, -3\}$
    \item $\{1, -2, 3\}$
    \item $\{-1, 2, 3\}$
    \item $\{-1, -2, -3\}$
\end{itemize}
For the set $\{1, 2, -3\}$, we have:
\begin{align*} r_1+r_2+r_3 &= 1+2-3 = 0 \\ r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3 &= (1)(2)+(1)(-3)+(2)(-3) = 2-3-6 = -7 \\ r_1r_2r_3 &= (1)(2)(-3) = -6\end{aligned}
So, $a=0$, $b=-7$, and $x^3-x^2-7x-6$ has roots $1, 2, -3$.
For the set $\{1, -2, 3\}$, we have:
\begin{align*} r_1+r_2+r_3 &= 1+(-2)+3 = 2 \\ r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3 &= (1)(-2)+(1)(3)+(-2)(3) = -2+3-6 = -5 \\ r_1r_2r_3 &= (1)(-2)(3) = -6\end{aligned}
So, $a=2$, $b=-5$, and $x^3+2x^2-5x-6$ has roots $1, -2, 3$.
For the set $\{-1, 2, 3\}$, we have:
\begin{align*} r_1+r_2+r_3 &= -1+2+3 = 4 \\ r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3 &= (-1)(2)+(-1)(3)+(2)(3) = -2-3+6 = 1 \\ r_1r_2r_3 &= (-1)(2)(3) = -6\end{aligned}
So, $a=4$, $b=1$, and $x^3+4x^2+x-6$ has roots $-1, 2, 3$.
For the set $\{-1, -2, -3\}$, we have:
\begin{align*} r_1+r_2+r_3 &= -1+(-2)+(-3) = -6 \\ r_1r_2+r_1r_3+r_2r_3 &= (-1)(-2)+(-1)(-3)+(-2)(-3) = 2+3+6 = 11 \\ r_1r_2r_3 &= (-1)(-2)(-3) = -6\end{aligned}
So, $a=-6$, $b=11$, and $x^3-6x^2+11x-6$ has roots $-1, -2, -3$.
The possible ordered pairs $(a,b)$ are $(0, -7)$, $(2, -5)$, $(4, 1)$, and $(-6, 11)$.

